From: Vorapol Rinsatitnon <vorapol.r@pm.me>
Date: Sun, 11 Oct 2026 21:42:52 +1300
Subject: [PATCH] internal/syscall/windows: wait out a just-run image where the kernel keeps it mapped

Windows 8 and 8.1 keep the image of a process mapped for a moment after
the process object is gone, up to a few tens of milliseconds. A delete in
that moment fails, the disposition with STATUS_CANNOT_DELETE and now and
then the open with a sharing violation. Windows 7, 10 and 11 release the
image before Wait returns. Go 1.20 slept 5 ms at the end of (*Process).Wait
on every Windows for this (issue 25965), and CL 509335 (Go 1.22) removed
the sleep because Windows 10 does not need it.

	process exits  ->  Wait returns  ->  image still mapped on 8 and 8.1
	                                 ->  the delete is refused for a moment

Deleteat tries again every millisecond for up to 100 ms when the open gets
a sharing violation or the disposition gets access denied, on those two
kernels only. The name has already been set aside by then, so the directory
empties at once and the file goes when the kernel lets it. A file that is
genuinely held costs 100 ms to fail there and nothing elsewhere. The sleep
Go 1.20 had would cost every process wait and still not cover Windows 8.

Nothing short of running a process can observe which kernels do this, so
the wait is gated on the kernel version, like 0004 and 0009.

